  "worked_solution": "First, we let\n\n$$\nI=\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}\u2061x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}\u2061x & \\,\u21d2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x} & \\,\u21d2\\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}\u2061x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}\u2061x(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{4})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}\u2061x}{4}-2\\int \\frac{{ln}^{2}\u2061x}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}\u2061x}{4}-2I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I,$ remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}\u2061x}{4}-2I \\\\ 3I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}\u2061x}{4} \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{3}\u2061x}{12}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
